      <!-- ───────── lede ───────── -->
      <p class="lede">
        <strong>Aness Bouziani</strong> is an <a>Algerian</a> <a>computer-science</a> student in his third year at the
        <a href="https://esi.dz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Higher School of Computer Science (ESI Algiers)</a>,
        with interests spanning <a>systems programming</a>, <a>mobile development</a>, <a>design and UI/UX</a>,
        and the quieter corners of how software is built and how people use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        He lives in <a>Algiers</a>, is active in the student-run <a>ETIC Club</a>, and is
        <strong>open to opportunities</strong> <a>remote-friendly</a> from Algeria.
      </p>

      <!-- ───────── Early life and education ───────── -->
      <h2 id="education">
        <span class="edit" data-edit="education" title="You don't have permission to edit this">[edit]</span>
        Early life and education
      </h2>
      <p>
        Aness is from <a>Algiers</a>, where he was born and raised. He completed a first
        <a>baccalauréat</a> in 2023 and spent the 2023–2024 academic year at the
        National Higher School of Autonomous Systems.<sup class="ref"><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup>
        In 2024 he sat a second <a>baccalauréat</a> — a path he describes, without much ceremony, as
        "a second key to the same door"<span class="cn-wrap"><span class="cn" data-note="Self-reported. Probably accurate. Probably also understated.">[citation needed]</span></span>
        — and enrolled the same year at the
        <a href="https://esi.dz" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Higher School of Computer Science (ESI Algiers)</a>
        as a third-year candidate, where he is currently pursuing computer science.
      </p>
      <p>
        He is currently in his <strong>third year</strong> at ESI, studying <a>computer science</a> with an interest
        in how systems are designed — the answers a curriculum gives and the questions it leaves unanswered.<sup class="ref"><a href="#ref-1">[1]</a></sup>
        He sees the degree less as a credential and more as a long, structured excuse to read, build, and break things
        in good company.<span class="cn-wrap"><span class="cn" data-note="His words, gently paraphrased.">[clarification needed]</span></span>
      </p>

      <!-- ─── Extracurricular ─── -->
      <h3 id="extracurricular" class="subhead">Extracurricular involvement</h3>
      <p>
        Since 2024 Aness has been an active member of the <a>ETIC Club</a>, ESI Algiers's student-run tech
        association. He builds, organizes, and ships with the club across the academic year. In 2026 he served as
        an <a>event coordinator</a> for the <a>S2EE 17</a> edition — one of the club's flagship events — handling
        programming, logistics, and the particular kind of calm that keeps a multi-track event on schedule.
        <sup class="ref"><a href="#ref-2">[2]</a></sup>
        <span class="cn-wrap"><span class="cn" data-note="The club runs events every year; the 17th edition is the one he coordinated.">[clarification needed]</span></span>
      </p>

      <!-- ───────── Interests ───────── -->
      <h2 id="interests">
        <span class="edit" data-edit="interests" title="You don't have permission to edit this">[edit]</span>
        Interests
      </h2>
      <p>
        Aness's interests sit at the intersection of <a>systems software</a>, <a>mobile development</a>,
        <a>design and UI/UX</a>, and <a>developer tooling</a> — he is drawn to problems where the user is also a
        builder, and where the right abstraction is a quiet one nobody notices. He cares as much about how a product
        feels to use as how it works underneath, and does his own design work rather than leaving it to someone else.
        He reads more documentation than is strictly required, keeps a long queue of side projects, and treats
        shipping small things as a way of learning a platform end-to-end instead of grazing its surface.<span class="cn-wrap"><span class="cn" data-note="He says. On the internet. Take with the usual grain of salt.">[citation needed]</span></span>
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside of software, he divides his attention between steady <a>caffeine</a> consumption, long walks through
        Algiers, and a stated preference for <a>house music</a> while debugging — a claim corroborated only by the
        Music app on his own <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.<sup class="ref"><a href="#ref-3">[3]</a></sup>
        He is, by his own account, <em>remote-friendly</em>, <em>open to opportunities</em>, and allergic to
        stand-ups that could have been an issue.
        <span class="cn-wrap"><span class="cn" data-note="Allergic in the figurative sense. No medical advice implied.">[citation needed]</span></span>
      </p>

      <!-- ───────── Approach ───────── -->
      <h2 id="approach">
        <span class="edit" data-edit="approach" title="You don't have permission to edit this">[edit]</span>
        Approach
      </h2>
      <p>
        Aness works alone well and with others better. He prefers reading the source over reading the docs,
        shipping over perfecting, and small utilities over big platforms. He keeps a side-project queue that grows
        faster than he can clear it, which he considers a feature rather than a bug. He has shipped work in
        <a>Swift</a>, <a>Dart</a>, <a>TypeScript</a>, and Python, and is quietly of the opinion that
        <a>Haskell</a> would have made him a better programmer if he had only taken the time.
        <span class="cn-wrap"><span class="cn" data-note="Everyone says this. Nobody takes the time.">[citation needed]</span></span>
      </p>
      <p>
        He believes the best portfolio is one the visitor remembers for what it felt like to use, not what it
        listed — which is, more or less, the reason this page is written like a Wikipedia article.
        <sup class="ref"><a href="#ref-4">[4]</a></sup>
      </p>

      <!-- ───────── Personal life ───────── -->
      <h2 id="personal">
        <span class="edit" data-edit="personal" title="You don't have permission to edit this">[edit]</span>
        Personal life
      </h2>
      <p>
        Aness lives in <a>Algiers</a>. He is reachable through his <a href="/contact">contact page</a> via email,
        GitHub, LinkedIn, Instagram, Facebook, WhatsApp, Discord, and the Algerian telephone network — a contact
        page styled as an iPhone home screen, because he thought it was funny and still does.<sup class="ref"><a href="#ref-3">[3]</a></sup>
        He does not run, does not cook as often as he would like, and keeps a long reading backlog he works through
        slowly and in no particular order. He is <strong>open to opportunities</strong> and takes the word
        <em>remote-friendly</em> literally.
      </p>

